  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\n- Benign weather through Tuesday with a dry cold frontal passage tonight\n\nA cold front is expected to cross the area tonight into Tuesday \nmorning across a northwest flow regime. Moisture is lacking in the \nlow-levels based on BUFKIT overviews supporting a dry forecast with \ncold fropa. Highs well into the 50s and possibly the low 60s in \nspots are favored.\n\n- System brings rain showers Wednesday followed by lake effect \n  rain/snow showers late week\n\nA clipper system moves south of the area Wednesday with increasing \nmoisture bringing rain showers beginning mid-day. Increasing \nnortherly flow behind this system brings 850mb temps down to near  \n-5C, which brings a 18-20C deltaT with current lake temps. Lake \neffect showers are expected, with FZLs as low as 2kft based on \nBUFKIT overviews suggesting rain will mix with snow at times. North \nto North-Northeast flow keeps the heaviest precipitation offshore, \nthough in the CWA the highest totals look to be near Big and Little \nSable points and near South Haven. Enhanced chance for snow to mix \nin will occur in these areas as well, as better forcing leads to \nhigher rates/better wet-bulbing. There will also be several \nshortwaves rounding the parent trough in place across the area that \nwould locally enhance precipitation based on placement and timing.\n\n- Conditions dry out this weekend \n\nAs of now, conditions are forecast to dry out going into the \nweekend, especially the late weekend. This will be driven by both \nsurface ridging and mid-level ridging/warm air advection ending \nmoisture flux for lake driven precipitation and subsidence. This \nwill also mark the beginning of a warmup back into the 50s after the \nnotably cooler mid to late week period.",
